      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To tackle this problem, let's break it down step-by-step.\n\n1. **Total Arrangements**:\n    - There are 9 seats, and we want to calculate the total possible ways they can sit.\n    - For the general case, calculate how many permutations exist when arranging \\( n \\) items around a round table.\n    - The formula for such permutations is given as \\( \\frac{n!}{n} \\) which simplifies to \\( (n - 1)! \\). In this case, \\( n = 9 \\):\n    \\[\n    (9 - 1)!\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    = 8!\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    = 40320\n    \\]\n\n2. **Favorable Arrangements**:\n    - We want the set consisting of 4 math majors to sit in consecutive seats.\n    - Treat the 4 consecutive math majors as one single block.\n    - In total, we have \\( 5 \\) blocks considering this block (each block excluding one narrower math major).\n    - This single block can sit in \\( 9 \\) ways by arranging 5 blocks, and we need to choose 4 positions for this block from 5 available slots.\n    \\[\n    \\binom{5}{4} \\cdot 9\n    \\]\n    - Hence, yielding:\n    \\[\n    = \\binom{5}{4} \\cdot 9\n    \\]\n    - Using the properties of combinations, we get:\n    \\[\n    = 5 \\cdot 9\n    \\]\n    - Hence, :\n    \\[\n    = 45\n    \\]\n\n3. **Calculating the Probability**:\n    - Finally, divide the number of favorable outcomes using these favorable scenarios (where math majors sit in a block) by all possible permutations.\n    \\[\n    \\frac{45}{40320}\n    \\]\n    - Simplify the fraction.\n\n    Simplifying further, calculating the greatest common divisor:\n    \\[\n    \\text{GCD}(45, 40320) = 45\n    \\]\n\n    So, our final required simplified probability of all math majors sitting consecutively:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{45}{40320} = \\frac{1}{896}\n    \\]\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{ \\frac{1}{896} }",
